## Migration: `Thread.Abort` → Cooperative Cancellation (.NET 8)

Your code uses the **timeout enforcement** pattern — aborting a thread that exceeds a time limit. In .NET 6+, `Thread.Abort()` throws `PlatformNotSupportedException` and `Thread.ResetAbort()` is removed entirely.

### The Problem

| .NET Framework API | .NET 8 Status |
|---|---|
| `Thread.Abort()` | Throws `PlatformNotSupportedException` (`SYSLIB0006`) |
| `Thread.ResetAbort()` | Removed — no-op / won't compile |
| `ThreadAbortException` | Never thrown by the runtime |

### Migrated Code

```csharp
public class TimedExecutor
{
    public T ExecuteWithTimeout<T>(Func<T> work, TimeSpan timeout)
    {
        using var cts = new CancellationTokenSource(timeout);
        var task = Task.Run(work, cts.Token);

        try
        {
            // Task.WaitAsync throws OperationCanceledException on timeout
            return task.WaitAsync(cts.Token).GetAwaiter().GetResult();
        }
        catch (OperationCanceledException) when (cts.IsCancellationRequested)
        {
            throw new TimeoutException(
                $"Operation exceeded {timeout.TotalSeconds}s timeout");
        }
        catch (AggregateException ex) when (ex.InnerExceptions.Count == 1)
        {
            throw new AggregateException(ex.InnerException!);
        }
    }
}
```

### Key Changes

1. **`CancellationTokenSource(timeout)`** replaces `Thread.Abort()` — auto-cancels after the deadline.
2. **`Task.Run`** replaces manual `Thread` creation — uses the thread pool.
3. **`Task.WaitAsync(token)`** (.NET 6+) replaces `Thread.Join(timeout)` — throws `OperationCanceledException` when cancelled.
4. **`catch (OperationCanceledException)`** replaces `catch (ThreadAbortException)`.
5. **`using var cts`** — `CancellationTokenSource` is `IDisposable`; leaking it causes timer leaks.

### Critical Pitfalls

| Pitfall | Detail |
|---|---|
| **Cancellation is cooperative, not preemptive** | Unlike `Thread.Abort`, the token won't interrupt a CPU-bound `Func<T>` mid-execution. If `work` is a tight loop, it must check `token.IsCancellationRequested` periodically. |
| **Fire-and-forget work continues** | After timeout, `Task.Run` keeps running in the background until it finishes or checks the token. The caller returns, but the work isn't killed. |
| **Blocking I/O isn't interrupted** | `Thread.Sleep`, `socket.Receive`, etc. won't respond to the token. Use async overloads (`await Task.Delay(ms, token)`, `await stream.ReadAsync(buf, token)`). |

### If `work` Must Be Cancellable

Change the signature to accept a `CancellationToken` so the work can cooperate:

```csharp
public T ExecuteWithTimeout<T>(Func<CancellationToken, T> work, TimeSpan timeout)
{
    using var cts = new CancellationTokenSource(timeout);
    var token = cts.Token;
    var task = Task.Run(() => work(token), token);

    try
    {
        return task.WaitAsync(token).GetAwaiter().GetResult();
    }
    catch (OperationCanceledException) when (cts.IsCancellationRequested)
    {
        throw new TimeoutException(
            $"Operation exceeded {timeout.TotalSeconds}s timeout");
    }
}

// Usage — work checks the token:
executor.ExecuteWithTimeout(token =>
{
    foreach (var item in items)
    {
        token.ThrowIfCancellationRequested();
        Process(item);
    }
    return results;
}, TimeSpan.FromSeconds(30));
```

### If Work is Truly Uncooperative (e.g., third-party code)

Move it to a **separate process** and use `Process.Kill()` — this is the only reliable way to forcibly terminate uncooperative code in modern .NET. There is no in-process equivalent to `Thread.Abort`.
